  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale:\n\nWe will employ a novel optimization strategy based on discrete calculus and adaptive neighborhood pruning. The core idea is to represent the sequence in a transformed space where we consider finite difference approximations and higher-order relations among coefficients. This differs significantly from the previous program which used interpolation in the time domain. We will:\n\n   a. Transform the sequence into a discrete space where neighboring coefficients are treated as independent variables\n   b. Use a gradient projection method tailored for sparse sequences of different lengths\n   c. Incorporate adaptive cooling when multiple structures show promise\n   d. Switch between structured grid search and unstructured mutation explosion during optimization phases\n\nThe main advantage is that this approach can better capture abrupt changes and discontinuities that might lead to improved step function properties. We will explore a range of sequence lengths and automatically normalize them to meet the evaluator's requirements. The algorithm will maintain a memory of promising sequence fragments across different optimizations.\n\n2. Mathematical Specification:\n\nWe define the search process in the following terms:\n\n   Objective: minimize J(a) = 2*n*max(convolve(a,a)) / (sum(a))^2\n   with constraints: each a[i] \u2265 0.0 and sum(a) \u2265 0.01\n\n   Transformation: \n      Let b[i] = a[i] - a[i-1] with boundary conditions defined such that the desired autocorrelation properties are maintained when back-transforming.\n\n   Optimization Formulations:\n      Primary Optimization: \n        Variables: b_vectors of bounded length\n        Objective: -\u221a(2*n) * ||b||_1 + k * max(convolve(a,a))\n        Constraints: lower_bound \u2264 b[i] \u2264 upper_bound for all i\n\n      Secondary Optimization:\n        We consider a dual form: \n           minimize_t J(t) = (1/t) * log(sum(exp(-t* convolve(a,a) / max_val))))\n\n      Steps for evaluation are clearly defined with FFT operations for obtaining the exact autocorrelation, with edge detection at specific lags to avoid circular convolution artifacts.\n\n   The algorithm must invalidate cached evaluations when the sequence length changes during transitions between optimization phases.\n\n3. Implementation Procedure:\n\nThe code structure will be:\n\n   ```python\n   import numpy as np\n   from scipy.optimize import minimize\n   from scipy.fftpack import rfft, rfftfreq, irfft\n   import time\n   \n   def evaluate_sequence(seq):\n       # Defend against empty/invalid inputs\n       if not isinstance(seq, list) or len(seq) < 1:\n           return np.inf\n       for x in seq:\n           if x < 0 or x > 1000 or not isinstance(x, float):\n               return np.inf\n       # Calculate exact convolution via FFT\n       n = len(seq)\n       signal = np.array(seq, dtype=np.float64)\n       convolution = rfft(signal) * rfft(signal[::-1])\n       # Compute maximum value properly\n       convolution_max = np.abs(irfft(convolution)).max()\n       signal_sum = np.sum(signal)\n       # Protection against invalid sums\n       if signal_sum < 0.01:\n           return np.inf\n       return float(2*n*convolution_max/(signal_sum**2))\n   \n   class SequenceOptimizer:\n       def __init__(self, budget=1500, max_length=2048, \n                   min_trim_factor=0.3, max_length_factor=1.5):\n           self.budget = budget  # In seconds\n           self.max_length = int(max_length)\n           self.min_trim_factor = min_trim_factor\n           self.max_length_factor = max_length_factor\n           # Time tracking components\n           self.start_time = time.time()\n           self.remaining_budget = budget - 30  # Reserve 30s for terminal fallback\n           # Candidate storage\n           self.best_sequence = None\n           self.best_score = float('inf')\n           # Sequence generation tools\n           self.trimmers = []\n           self.lengths = [512, 1024, 2048]\n           self.phase_time = 0.0\n           \n       def generate_candidate_perturbations(self, seq, num_perts=10):\n           # Perturbative transformation in discrete space\n           n_new = int(len(seq) * 1.1)\n           n_current = len(seq)\n           # Ensure numeric stability\n           np.clip(seq, 0.001, 1000, out=seq)\n           perts = []\n           for _ in range(num_perts):\n               # Create truncated+renormalized variant\n               if n_current < self.min_trim_factor * n_new:\n                   continue\n               t = np.linspace(0, 1, n_new)\n               # Optional: Generate both trimmer and resampler versions\n               candidate = np.interp(t, np.linspace(0, 1, n_current), seq)\n               candidate = candidate / candidate.sum()\n               # Apply detonation transformation\n               delta_seq = candidate.copy()\n               index = rng.randint(0, n_new)\n               delta_seq[index] = (rng.random() - 0.5) * 10\n               delta_seq = np.clip(delta_seq, 0.001, 1000)\n               perts.append(delta_seq)\n           return perts\n   ```\n\n4. Search choices:\n\nPhase sequencing:\n\n   Phase 1: Coordinate finder\n   - Search grid widths: 256, 512, 1024, 2048 (auto adjusting based on domain)\n   - Description: Explore different grid structures independently and maintain the best candidate from any grid.\n\n   Phase 2: Local minimizer\n   - Switch to gradient projection method\n   - Armijo linesearch with barrier functions for negative values\n\n   Phase 3: Multi-start stair climbing\n   - Temperature schedule: [80, 40, 20, 10]\n   - Requires random index perturbations at each annealing point\n\n5. Budget management:\n\nTotal execution time budget: 1500 seconds\n\nWe divide the budget into four phases:\n\n   Phase 1: 25% of budget\n   Phase 2: 50% of budget\n   Phase 3: 20% of budget\n   Phase 4: 5% of budget for final stabilization\n\nFor each sequence length, the algorithm will budget a fraction based on the length (shorter sequences get more wall-clock time)\n\n6. Validation and output:\n\nCandidates are evaluated and stored if:\n\n   1. They are not already stored (we maintain a separate best candidate array)\n   2. Their convolution maximum is properly defined (edge cases handled)\n   3. They have a positive sum (time measurement during convolution avoids invalid values)\n   4. They show improvement (J(a) < current threshold)\n\nFallback position should be a normalized sequence of ones with length optimized dynamically.",
